# In Situ Polymerization of Cross‐Linked Perovskite–Polymer Composites for Highly Stable and Efficient Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Mixed‐halide perovskites have emerged as outstanding light absorbers that enable the fabrication of efficient solar cells; however, their instability hinders the commercialization of such systems. Grain‐boundary (GB) defects and lattice tensile strain are critical intrinsic‐instability factors in polycrystalline perovskite films. In this study, the light‐induced cross‐linking of acrylamide (Am) monomers with non‐crystalline perovskite films is used to fabricate highly efficient and stable perovskite solar cells (PSCs). The Am monomers induce the preferred crystal orientation in the polycrystalline perovskite films, enlarge the perovskite grain size, and cross‐link the perovskite grains. Additionally, the liquid properties of Am effectively releases lattice strain during perovskite‐film crystallization. The cross‐linked interfacial layer functions as an airtight wall that protects the perovskite film from water corrosion. Devices fabricated using the proposed strategy show an excellent power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 24.45% with an open‐circuit voltage (VOC) of 1.199 V, which, to date, is the highest VOC reported for hybrid PSCs with electron transport layers (ETLs) comprised of TiO2. Large‐area PSC modules fabricated using the proposed strategy show a power conversion efficiency of 20.31% (with a high fill factor of 77.1%) over an active area of 33 cm2, with excellent storage stability.

Mixed-halide perovskites have emerged as outstanding light absorbers that enable the fabrication of efficient solar cells; however, their instability hinders the commercialization of such systems. Grain-boundary (GB) defects and lattice tensile strain are critical intrinsic-instability factors in polycrystalline perovskite films. In this study, the light-induced cross-linking of acrylamide (Am) monomers with non-crystalline perovskite films is used to fabricate highly efficient and stable perovskite solar cells (PSCs). The Am monomers induce the preferred crystal orientation in the polycrystalline perovskite films, enlarge the perovskite grain size, and cross-link the perovskite grains. Additionally, the liquid properties of Am effectively releases lattice strain during perovskite-film crystallization. The cross-linked interfacial layer functions as an airtight wall that protects the perovskite film from water corrosion. Devices fabricated using the proposed strategy show an excellent power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $24.45\%$ with an open-circuit voltage $(\boldsymbol{v}_{\mathrm{{oc}}})$ of 1.199 V, which, to date, is the highest $\pmb{v_{\mathrm{oc}}}$ reported for hybrid PSCs with electron transport layers (ETLs) comprised of $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ . Large-area PSC modules fabricated using the proposed strategy show a power conversion efficiency of $20.31\%$ (with a high fill factor of $77.1\%$ over an active area of $33c m^{2}$ , with excellent storage stability.
## 1. Introduction
Solution-processed hybrid organic–inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted significant attention as low-cost and highly efficient photovoltaic technology.[1–3] Although the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of PSCs is comparable to that of commercial-silicon solar cells,[4] the poor intrinsic stability and high sensitivity of PSCs to moisture, external bias, and light limit their commercialization.[5,6] This instability can be primarily attributed to surface/interface defects and grain boundaries (GBs) generated on solutionprocessed polycrystalline perovskite films.[7,8] Notably, GBs significantly influence the optoelectronic properties of perovskites. For example, compared with polycrystalline perovskites with multiple GBs, single-crystal perovskites exhibit a significantly longer diffusion length $(10{-}100~\upmu\mathrm{m})^{[9]}$ and lower defect density $(10^{9}–10^{10}~\mathrm{~cm}^{-3})$ .[10] GBs with defects and dangling bonds generate alternative ion-migration pathways, reduce the quasi-Fermi-level splitting, and cause significant non-radiative recombination, which reduces device performance.[11–13] Furthermore, moisture and oxygen permeate the defective regions along GBs, resulting in rapid device degradation under ambient conditions.[7,14,15]
For GB-defect modulation, several studies have attempted to passivate defects and enlarge grain sizes via chemical interactions with metal oxides,[16] small molecules,[17] and longchain polymers.[18,19] However, metal oxides typically exhibit a disordered distribution in GBs, increasing their compositional complexity. Small molecules with lone-pair electrons suppress GB defects and improve the optoelectronic properties of perovskite films. However, they adversely affect the device stability under realistic environments owing to their high volatility and diffusion coefficients.[20,21] Several other strategies (including the surface-immobilization of long-chain polymers by crosslinking) have been used to improve the photophysical stability of perovskite films.[22–24] However, these GB-defect-passivation strategies involve specific reactions (such as chemical bonding, cross-linking, and polymerization) that occur during perovskitefilm crystallization, which adversely affects the suppression of residual-strain-induced lattice distortion in microcrystal perovskite films.[7,25]
Lattice strain in polycrystalline perovskite films—caused by a mismatch of the thermal expansion coefficients of the perovskite material and substrate—accelerates perovskite decomposition by reducing the ion-migration activation energy.[8,26] Several strategies (such as compositional alloying by sequential A-site doping,[27] thermal-expansion regulation by annealingprocess modification,[28] interface post-treatment,[29] and chargetransport-layer formation)[26] have been used to minimize this strain.[25–34] However, the process of these methods is difficult to control and may cause only partial perovskite-film relaxation; additionally, they do not consider GB modifications, which significantly influence the properties of perovskite systems.
Here, highly efficient and stable PSCs were constructed using a strategy involving acrylamide (Am) treatment before perovskite crystallization, and light-induced cross-linking (ABC strategy). Am monomers induced the preferred crystal orientation in polycrystalline perovskite films, enlarged the perovskite grain size, and cross-linked the perovskite grains, passivating the perovskite-film defects by carboxyl-group coordination. Additionally, the liquidity of Am effectively released the lattice strain generated during perovskite-film crystallization. Light-induced Am polymerization formed a protective “wall” with waterproof properties on the perovskite films. A PSC fabricated using the proposed strategy showed an excellent PCE of $24.45\%$ with an opencircuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of 1.199 V; this is the highest $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ recorded for hybrid PSCs with electron transport layers (ETLs) comprising $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Table S1, Supporting Information). Moreover, as-prepared PSCs without encapsulation showed excellent stability, retaining $80\%$ of the initial PCE after $700\mathrm{~h~}$ under $55\pm5\%$ moisture or 1008 h under light. Large-area PSC modules fabricated using the proposed strategy showed a PCE of $20.31\%$ (with a significantly high fill factor (FF) of $77.1\%$ over an active area of $33\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ .
## 2. Results and Discussion
Am, as the light-triggered polymerizable monomers, is chosen to control defect passivation, crystal orientation, grain size, and strain of perovskite film. Polyacrylamide (PAm), with high surface activity, hydrophilicity, optimum hygroscopicity, and nontoxicity, is widely used in industries.[35] Here, perovskite films after/before crystallization (by thermal annealing) were spincoated by an Am solution (Figure 1). Coating crystalline perovskite films with Am caused only surface passivation, indicating similar (random) crystal orientations and grain sizes in crystalline perovskite films with and without Am treatment (Figure 1a). Contrarily, coating non-crystalline perovskite films with Am affected the nucleation and growth of defects during perovskite-film crystallization, generating large and isolated perovskite grains (Figure 1b). Additionally, the liquidity of Am minimized lattice distortions in Am-coated non-crystalline perovskite films during crystallization (due to heating and cooling), facilitating efficient strain relaxation. Notably, the waterproof PAm layer formed by a light-induced process significantly improved the device stability under harsh environmental conditions (Figure S1, Supporting Information).[36]
Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was used to investigate the polymerization of Am molecules before and after the light-curing process. As shown in Figure 2a, the spectrum of $\mathrm{Am}@\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ measured under dark conditions contains multiple peaks in the frequency range of $1000{-}900\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to vinyl groups, similar to the spectrum of Am. After illumination for $10\mathrm{min}$ , the peaks corresponding to ${\mathrm{CH}}{=}\mathrm{CH}$ disappear, indicating the polymerization of Am to PAm. Additionally, Figure 2b indicates that the amide-group $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ coordinates with $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . The peak corresponding to the amide-group $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching appears at $1676~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in the spectrum of neat Am; this peak is shifted to lower wavenumbers of 1654 and $1646~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in the spectra of $\mathrm{Am}@\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ (dark) and $\mathbf{Am}@\mathbf{Pb}\mathbf{I}_{2}$ (light), respectively, indicating coordination between $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and the amide-group $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ .[37–39] Additionally, the peak corresponding to the bending vibration of the amide-group $\tt N-H$ bond appears at ${\approx}1544\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in the spectrum of neat Am. In the solid state, hydrogen bonding occurs between the ${\tt N}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{H}}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ moieties of the amide group. On mixing $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , the $\delta_{\mathrm{N-H}}$ peak is red shifted to $1510\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , indicating amide– $\mathrm{.PbI}_{2}$ interactions that decrease the hydrogen bonding.[36,40]
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to investigate the interactions between Am and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ further. Pb 4f XPS spectra (Figure 2c) indicate that the peaks at 138.1 and $143.0\mathrm{eV}$ in the spectrum of the control film (corresponding to Pb $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ of the Pb─I species, respectively) are shifted to lower binding energies (138 and $142.9\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively) in the spectra of films containing Am; this confirms the reduction of uncoordinated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and passivation.[41,42] Additionally, peaks corresponding to $\mathrm{~I~}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $[3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ at 618.91 and $630.39~\mathrm{eV},$ respectively, in the spectrum of the control film are shifted to lower binding energies (618.75 and $630.24\:\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively) in the spectrum of films containing Am, indicating a reduction in the oxidation state of iodide or a weakening of the Pb─I bond owing to interactions with the ammonium cation (Figure S2, Supporting Information). Therefore, the XPS and FTIR results both confirm strong Am– $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ coordination.
To analyze the ABC strategy, the structural properties of perovskite films fabricated by no Am treatment (i.e., control perovskite films), Am treatment before perovskite crystallization combined with light-induced cross-linking (i.e., ABC-treated perovskite films), and Am treatment after crystallization with lightinduced cross-linking (i.e., AAC-treated perovskite films) were investigated by high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and X-ray diffraction (XRD). Figure 2d–f indicates that perovskites fabricated by the ABC process show a grain size of ${\approx}755\mathrm{nm}$ , whereas the AAC-treated and control samples show a similar grain size of ${\approx}430\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Thus, perovskite films fabricated by the ABC strategy undergo controlled crystal growth owing to chemical interactions between organic/Pb cations and Am, chelation, and hydrogen bonding, which results in the formation of large-size grains.[37,43] In addition, the interfacial polyacrylamide layer as an amorphous phase presented in the interface between perovskite grain boundaries is observed in the HRTEM image (Figure S4, Supporting Information). Among all the XRD patterns, those of ABC-treated perovskite films (Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ and Figure S5, Supporting Information) exhibit the strongest (001) peak intensity and smallest full width at half maximum (FWHM), indicating that the ABC strategy yields perovskite films with the highest crystallinity and largest grain size. The XRD results are consistent with those of SEM.
Water contact-angle measurements were used to investigate the moisture resistance of the perovskite films (Figure S6, Supporting Information). As expected, the water contact angles of Am-treated perovskite films after light-induced polymerization $(\approx56^{\circ})$ were significantly greater than that of the control perovskite film $(25.6^{\circ})$ , regardless of AAC or ABC treatment. Therefore, the formation of PAm by light-induced cross-linking efficiently enhanced the perovskite-film hydrophobicity. Subsequently, a water-tolerance experiment was used to evaluate the long-term water resistance of the ABC-treated perovskite films. On contact with a water droplet, the color of the control perovskite film changed from black to yellow; contrarily, the ABCtreated perovskite film did not change its intrinsic color, even on soaking in water (Figure S7 and Movie S1, Supporting Information).
Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectroscopy were used to investigate the charge-transfer behavior of the Am-treated perovskite films. The PL intensities of the AAC- and ABC-treated perovskite films were 1.45- and 3.53-fold greater than that of the control film, respectively, indicating a significant suppression of nonradiative recombination in Am-treated perovskite films (Figure 2h). Figure 2i shows the fitting of the TRPL data to a biexponential decay equation (the fitting results are summarized in Table S2, Supporting Information). AAC-treated perovskite films showed an average lifetime of $94.90\mathrm{ns}$ , which is significantly longer than that of the control $(40.12\mathrm{ns})$ . Furthermore, ABC-treated films showed a significantly prolonged lifetime of 239.02 ns, indicating a low defect density.
The crystallization behavior and defect-passivation mechanism of ABC-treated perovskite films are shown in Figure 3a,b. Am molecules efficiently interacted with non-crystalline perovskite films at the GBs and the surface. Strong interactions between the $-\mathrm{C}=0$ group and the Pb─I framework caused a decomposition of the large-scale Pb─I structure in wet films, promoting crystal growth in the preferred orientation.[40] Subsequently, the Am molecules formed a polymer network under light illumination, inducing efficient defect passivation at the GBs and surface of the perovskite films.[7,44] Contrarily, Am molecules did not exhibit deep penetration into the GBs of crystallized perovskite films; therefore, after polymerization, they simply formed a polymer network on the surface of such films (Figure 3a).[7] Figure 3b shows the cross-sectional SEM images of the control, AAC-treated, and ABC-treated perovskite films. The control and AAC-treated perovskite films exhibit irregular and vertically disconnected crystals, whereas the ABC-treated perovskite film contains regular and isolated crystal grains (which increase in size with the Am concentration). The large and isolated crystal grains in ABC-treated perovskite films influence the vertical diffusivity of charge carriers;[45] as each vertical GB acts as an energy barrier, this indicates enhanced charge-collection properties.
Grazing incidence X-ray diffraction (GIXRD) with the $2\theta-$ $\sin^{2}\psi$ method was used to investigate the effect of residual strain on the as-prepared perovskite films. As shown in Figure 3c, the characteristic (022) diffraction peaks in the control perovskite film are shifted to lower angles on changing $\psi$ from $0^{\circ}$ to $50^{\circ}$ , indicating an increase in the crystal-plane distance (according to Bragg’s law) and tensile strain. The AAC-treated perovskite films show similar results (Figure 3d). Interestingly, the ABCtreated perovskite films show a negligible shift in lattice spacing, regardless of the $\psi$ angle, indicating no residual stresses inside the film (Figure 3e). To investigate the residual stress in the perovskite films further, the lattice strain was calculated by fitting $2\theta$ as a function of $\sin^{2}\psi$ , as shown in Figure 3f (details are provided in Note S1, Supporting Information).[29] In these plots, the control and AAC-treated perovskite films exhibit significant changes in the slope with the diffraction angle, exhibiting residual tensile strains of 51.9 and $36.5~\mathrm{MPa}$ , respectively (Figure 3f inset). However, the ABC-treated perovskite films exhibit a residual tensile strain of $7.44~\mathrm{MPa}$ , which is 4.9 times lower than that of AAC-treated perovskite films. Thus, the liquidity of Am facilitates efficient stress release, minimizing lattice distortion during perovskite-film crystallization (as discussed above in Figure 1).
Space-charge-limited current (SCLC) measurements using electron-only $\mathrm{(FTO/TiO_{2}/P V K/C_{60}/A u)}$ and hole-only devices (FTO/PTAA/PVK/Spiro) were used to evaluate the trap density $(n_{\mathrm{trap}})$ of the devices (Figure 3g,h). The $n_{\mathrm{trap}}$ values were determined from the trap-filled voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ evaluated using the following: $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}=e n_{\mathrm{trap}}L^{2}/(2\varepsilon\varepsilon_{\mathrm{0}})$ , where $L$ is the thickness of the perovskite film $(500\ \mathrm{nm})$ , ɛ is the relative dielectric constant of perovskite (30.8), $\ensuremath{\varepsilon}_{0}$ is the vacuum permittivity $(8.85\times10^{-14}\mathrm{~F~cm^{-1}})$ ), and $e$ is the elementary charge. The control, AAC-treated, and ABC-treated devices exhibited electron$n_{\mathrm{trap}}$ values of $7.21\times10^{15}$ , $6.5\times10^{15}$ , and $5.4\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively, and hole- ${\cdot n_{\mathrm{trap}}}$ values of $6.94\times10^{15}$ , $5.03\times10^{15}$ , and $2.17\times10^{15}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. Among all the tested devices, ABCtreated perovskite devices showed the lowest $n_{\mathrm{trap}}$ values, consistent with the PL and TRPL results. This can be attributed to the large grain size, low strain, and defect passivation effects in this film.
Figure 4a shows the $\mathrm{FTO/TiO_{2}/(F A P b I_{3})_{0.95}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.05}/}$ Spiro-OMeTAD/Au devices used to verify the photovoltaic performance of the as-prepared perovskite films. Figure S8, Supporting Information, summarizes the statistics of the photovoltaic parameters including the PCE, $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ , short-circuit current density $(J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}})$ , and FF. The average PCE of the control, AAC-treated, and ABC-treated devices are $22.1\%$ , $23.2\%$ , and $24\%$ , respectively. The $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF values of the AAC- and ABC-treated devices are significantly greater than those of the control device. The average $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF of the ABC-treated device are $6\%$ and $4.3\%$ greater than those of the control device, respectively.
To elucidate this improvement in performance, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ was plotted as a function of the light intensity (Figure 4b). The curves of the control, AAC-treated, and ABC-treated PCSs show slopes of 1.64, 1.42, and $1.21~k_{\mathrm{B}}/T q$ , respectively; $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant, T is the absolute temperature in kelvin, and $\boldsymbol{q}$ is the elementary charge. The low slope of the curve plotted for ABCtreated devices indicates the effective suppression of trap-assisted recombination in these systems.[46,47]
To investigate changes in the electrical properties of the perovskite films owing to PAm passivation, the Fermi level and band edge were calculated from ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) and UV absorbance data (Figures S9 and S10, Supporting Information). Figure 4c indicates that the Fermi level and band edge of ABC-treated perovskite films are shifted to $+0.2$ and $0.17\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, minimizing injection-energy losses.[41,48] Subsequently, the capacitance–voltage $(C-V)$ curves (Figure 4d) and built-in potential $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ values of the as-prepared devices were compared. The $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ values were estimated by the Mott–Schottky equation, as follows
$$
\frac{1}{C^{2}}=\frac{2\left(V_{\mathrm{bi}}-\nu\right)}{A^{2}q\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}N_{\mathrm{A}}}
$$
where A is the device area, $N_{\mathrm{A}}$ is the carrier concentration, V is the applied bias, $C$ is the capacitance, ɛ is the relative dielectric, and $\mathfrak{\varepsilon}_{0}$ is the vacuum permittivity.[49] Figure 4d indicates that the $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ of the ABC-treated device (1.13 V) is significantly greater than that of the control device (0.91 V), possibly owing to efficient defect passivation and low energy losses in the former.[49]
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was used to investigate charge-carrier recombination in the as-prepared PSCs (Figure 4e and Table S3, Supporting Information). The ABCtreated devices showed a low series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ ; this can be attributed to an enhanced FF owing to the presence of vertically oriented crystal grains in the perovskite films. Moreover, they showed the highest recombination resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ among all the tested devices, indicating that the ABC-treated devices showed a relatively lower charge-carrier recombination rate at the interface.[50]
Figure 4f shows the current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the optimal control, AAC-treated, and ABC-treated devices; Table 1 summarizes their characteristics. The best performing “champion” ABC-treated device shows a maximum PCE of $24.45\%$ , with a $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ of $25.18~\mathrm{mA~cm^{-2}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.199\mathrm{~V},$ FF of $81\%$ , and negligible hysteresis $(1.6\%)$ ; 1.199 V is the highest $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ reported for organic–inorganic hybrid PSCs with ETLs comprising $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Notably, the integrated $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ values of the control, AAC-treated, and ABC-treated devices estimated from external quantum-efficiency spectra are consistent with the experimentally measured values of $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Subsequently, the steady-state PCEs of the as-prepared devices were investigated at the maximum power point and under continuous illumination in ambient air (without encapsulation, at a relative humidity [RH] of $\approx10\%$ , and ${\approx}45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Figure S12, Supporting Information, indicates that the ABC-treated devices exhibit excellent operational stability, maintaining $95\%$ of their original efficiency after $7300\mathrm{s}$ .
To confirm the environmental stability of the ABC-treated devices, their performance was monitored without encapsulation under harsh environmental stresses (such as light, heat, and moisture). The performance of the control and AAC-treated devices deteriorated rapidly under harsh conditions. Contrarily, the ABC-treated devices showed excellent light stability, maintaining $80\%$ of their initial PCE after $1008\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}$ ), possibly owing to ion-migration suppression in the perovskite films, which passivated the GB defects and released residual strain in the films.[25,51] Figure 4h shows the moisture stability of unencapsulated devices upon exposure to an RH of ${\approx}55\ {\pm}5\%$ at room temperature $\scriptstyle(\approx20\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ in a dark commercial cabinet. The moisture stability of the control device starts to decrease within the first few days, and it stops working after $220\mathrm{~h~}$ ; contrarily, the AAC- and ABC-treated devices show $73\%$ and $80\%$ performance retention, respectively, after $700\mathrm{{h}}$ . These results confirm the waterproof properties of crosslinked PAm, which protects the AACand ABC-treated systems from water and oxygen under harsh conditions.
Subsequently, perovskite solar modules (PSMs) with an area of $36\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ were fabricated (Figure S13, Supporting Information) and the detailed fabrication process is illustrated in Figure S14, Supporting Information; Figures S15 and S16, Supporting Information, show details regarding their design. The PSMs contain ten sub-cells (in an aperture area of $36~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) connected with typical P1, P2, and P3 etching lines. Optical-microscope measurements of the etching regions (Figure 4i) indicate that the PSMs exhibit a geometrical fill factor (GFF) of $91.67\%$ ( ${\mathrm{GFF}}=.$ active area divided by the aperture area). Figure 4j shows the $J{-}V$ curves of the bestperforming control, AAC-treated, and ABC-treated PSMs; the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table S4, Supporting Information. The FF and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of PSC modules were slightly decreased compared to those of champion smallarea devices. This is primarily due to the higher heat accumulation resulting from large-area modules, which, in turn, leads to increased charge carrier recombination. Additionally, scribing on large-area FTO substrates with lower conductivity remains a big challenge. The best-performing ABC-treated PSM exhibits a PCE of $18.62\%$ with an excellent FF of $77.1\%$ over an aperture area of $36~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ (with a PCE of $20.31\%$ over an active area of $33~\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ). These results confirm that the ABC process is suitable for the fabrication of scalable PSMs. Additionally, the corresponding steady-state PCE of the ABC-treated module was tested under illumination in ambient air (under an RH in the range of $5-10\%$ ; Figure S17, Supporting Information). The optimal ABC-treated module shows a stabilized PCE of $20.05\%$ (with an aperture area of $18.37\%)$ over 3100 s of tracking at a bias voltage of $9.46\mathrm{V}$ and outstanding storage stability (with a retention of $88.9\%$ of its initial efficiency after 2000 h) (Figure 4k).
## 3. Conclusion
This study reports a novel and efficient strategy (i.e., the ABC method) for fabricating highly efficient and stable PSCs using the light-induced cross-linking of Am monomers with noncrystalline perovskite films. This strategy can not only release the residual strain but also reconstruct the perovskite crystallization and stabilize the GBs. The ABC method enables the fabrication of high-quality perovskite films with multiple functionalities (including large grains in a preferred orientation, moisture repulsion, trap-density reduction, and suppressed trap-assisted recombination). ABC-treated devices show a significantly high $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and PCE $(24.45\%)$ , and ABC-treated PSCs exhibit high moisture stability (with the retention of $80\%$ of the initial PCE after $700\mathrm{~h~}$ ) and light stability (with the retention of $80\%$ of the initial PCE after $1008\mathrm{h}$ ). Moreover, ABC-treated large-area PSC modules exhibit a PCE of $20.31\%$ (with a high FF of $77.1\%$ over an active area of $33\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ ) and excellent storage stability. The ABC strategy proposed in this study facilitates a synergetic enhancement of the stability and efficiency of PSCs opening new frontiers in PSC research.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Fluorine-doped tin oxide $(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ : F, FTO) was purchased from Pilkington (TEC8, 8 $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{\Omega}}^{\prime}\ \mathsf{s q}^{-1})$ ). Titanium tetrachloride $(\mathsf{T i C l}_{4})$ , dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.9\%$ ), acetonitrile $(99.8\%)$ , dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ , chlorobenzene (CB, $99\%$ ), diethyl ether $(99.7\%)$ , 1,4-butanesultone (Sigma-Aldrich, $99\%$ ). 2,4,6-trimethylbenzoyldiphenyl phosphine oxide (TPO, Sigma-Aldrich), poly [bis (4-phenyl) (2,4,6- trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA, $M_{\mathfrak{n}}=3200$ , $M_{\mathrm{w}}=4900)$ , and 6,6-phenyl C61 butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) were purchased from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp (China). Acrylamide, $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(99.995\%)$ , $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ $(99\%)$ , ${\mathsf{H C}}({\mathsf{N H}}_{2})_{2}$ (FAI), ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{B r}}$ (MABr), methylammonium chloride (MACl), lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide salt (Li-TFSI), and 4-tert-butylpyridine (tBP) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (USA). $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -Tetrakis(N,N-di-p-methoxyphenylamine)-9,9′-spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD, LT-S922) was purchased from Luminescence Technology Corp. All materials were purchased from commercial sources and used without further purification.
Device Fabrication: Perovskite precursor solutions comprising $(F A P b|_{3})_{0.95}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.05}$ $(7.6~\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{M})$ were prepared by dissolving FAI (0.263 g), MABr (0.009 g), $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(0.726\ \ g)$ , and $\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ (0.029 g) in $7m L$ of a DMSO/DMF (8/2) mixed solvent containing excess $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ (in a molar ratio of $3\%$ ). To prepare HTM solutions, spiro-OMeTAD $(90~\mathsf{m g})$ , tBP $(36~\upmu\up L)$ , and Li-TFSI in acetonitrile $(23\upmu\up L$ ; $520~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ ) were dissolved in $7m L$ of CB.
The cross-linked precursor solution was synthesized by dissolving TPO powder $(5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ (as an initiator) in a solution containing Am with roomtemperature stirring. IPA dilution was used to prepare different concentrations of the precursor solution.
FTO-coated glass substrates were etched by laser irradiation and then cleaned by sonication with deionized water, ethanol, and acetone for 15 min each. An aqueous $\mathsf{T i C l}_{4}$ stock solution $(2M)$ was prepared by mixing $\mathsf{T i C l}_{4}$ with deionized water in an ice bath and stored in a refrigerator at $-4^{\circ}\mathsf C;$ it remained stable for at least 3 months. Subsequently, a compact layer of $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was deposited on the substrate by chemical bath deposition. A clean FTO substrate was briefly soaked in aqueous $\mathsf{T i C l}_{4}$ $(0.78~\mathsf{M})$ , placed in a sealed glass container, and dried in an oven at $70^{\circ}C$ for 45 min. After cooling, the FTO substrate was rinsed with ethanol and deionized water thrice to remove any residual $\mathsf{T i C l}_{4}$ . Subsequently, it was annealed at 200 $^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $\rceil\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ and then cooled to room temperature $(\approx20~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ under ambient conditions. The perovskite precursor solution was spin-coated on this substrate at 5000 rpm for 25 s. 20 s after the commencement of the spin process, ether $(0.5~\mathsf{m L})$ was rapidly dropped onto the rotating substrate, which was then annealed at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ . To fabricate ABC-treated devices, this wet film was transferred to a hot plate and annealed at $80^{\circ}C$ for $\mathsf{70}\mathsf{s}$ . After cooling, the cross-linked precursor solution was spin-coated on the perovskite film at $5000~\mathsf{r p m}$ for $25\ \mathsf{s},$ which was then annealed at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ and irradiated by sunlight for $10\min$ . To fabricate AACtreated samples, the cross-linked precursor solution was spin-coated on the perovskite film at 5000 rpm for 25 s and then irradiated by sunlight for 10 min. A large-sized (with a diameter of $5c m$ ) spin-coater chuck was used for coating large-area $(7\times7\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ substrates, which were placed at the center of the chuck to avoid machine damage due to high-speed coating. The perovskite precursor solution $(0.75~\mathrm{mL})$ was dropped on fixed substrates and uniformly spread using a pipette tip $(7m L)$ . During the coating process, $3m L$ of an antisolvent (anisole) was dropped on the substrate by a $5.5\cdot\mathsf{m L}$ pipette (using a plastic cover to avoid the random splashing of the solution). Annealing the resultant transparent, crack-free, and brown wet film on a hotplate at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ generated a uniform and mirrorlike perovskite large-area film. HTM $(40~\upmu\up L)$ was deposited on this film by spin-coating at 3500 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . Finally, a $40-\mathsf{n m}$ -thick Au-electrode metal layer was deposited on the top surface of this device; this layer was thermally evaporated at $0.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ to form the back contact.
Device Characterization: The J–V characteristics of the as-prepared solar cells were measured using a solar simulator (Newport Oriel Solar 3A Class AAA, 64023A) and potentiostat (CHI 660D, CH instruments) under 1 sun illumination of the AM $\mathsf{1.5~C}$ spectrum $(100\ m A\ c m^{-2}$ ). The potentiostat was calibrated using a standard Si-based solar cell (Oriel, VLSI standards) and a light-sensor current controller (Newport Oriel digital exposure controller, Model 68 945). The performances of all the devices were measured after covering the active area with a thin mask $(0.74\ c m^{2})$ ). $J-V$ curves were measured using reverse (R) and forward (F) scanning at a rate of $200\ m\vee\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ under 1 sun illumination of the simulated AM $\mathsf{I}.5\mathsf{C}$ spectrum $(700\mathsf{m}\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2})$ in ambient air (room temperature $(\approx20^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ and an RH in the range of $5-10\%$ ; all the devices were illuminated under 1 sun for $20-30~\mathsf{s}$ before $J{-}V$ measurements. PL spectra were recorded using a fluorescence lifetime spectrometer (Quantaurus Tau C11367-12, HAMAMATSU) with 464-nm pulsed (10 MHz)-laser (PLP-10, HAMAMATSU) excitation. TRPL (measured by the time-correlated single-photon counting technique) was used to analyze the spontaneous PL decay. SEM images and elemental-content mappings were recorded by a hot-field emission scanning electron microscope (JSM-7600F, JEOL, Japan). XRD patterns of the perovskite films were measured using a D8 Discover instrument with $\mathsf{C u K}\alpha$ radiation $(\lambda=7.5478\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ). Absorbance spectra were measured using a UV–vis spectrometer (Lambda 45, Perkin Elmer). An ESCALAB 250 XPS system (Thermo Fisher Scientific) with Al ${\sf K}\alpha$ X-ray radiation (1486.6 eV) was used for XPS. A 21.22-eV light source was used for UPS. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were recorded using JEM-ARM200F, Japan. TEM samples were prepared by spin-casting the perovskite solution $(\lnot\bowtie)$ onto holey carbon TEM grids. Note that during the whole process, the TEM grids were in a glass Petri dish to avoid possible damage to the grids. An IPCE system for photovoltaic measurements in the DC mode with a monochromatic beam generated by a 75-W Xe lamp (USHIO) was used for IPCE spectral measurements. A Bruker D8 Advance system with a Cu K𝛼 source $(8.05\mathrm{keV},1.54\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ and an incidence angle of $0.5^{\circ}$ was used for GIXRD.
